{
  "id": "adjektivdeklination-a2",
  "name": "Adverbs in -ment: lentement, heureusement; bon or bien, mauvais or mal",
  "level": "A2",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "variety": "fr-FR",
  "subtitle": "Add -ment to the feminine form of an adjective to make an adverb: lente → lentement, heureuse → heureusement. Adverbs describe a verb: Il parle lentement. Adjectives describe a noun: C'est un bon médecin, but Il travaille bien.",
  "briefMarks": ["-ment", "-amment", "-emment", "vraiment", "poliment", "bien", "mal", "vite", "bon", "mauvais"],
  "source": "icanspeak French original text (grammar-a2 run, 2026-11-08)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [{ "text": "adjective, feminine", "variant": "verb" }, { "text": "+ -ment", "variant": "prep" }, { "text": "adverb" }],
    "exampleHtml": "lent<span class=\"vp-verb\">e</span> → lent<span class=\"vp-prep\">ement</span>: Il parle lentement. · heureu<span class=\"vp-verb\">se</span> → heureu<span class=\"vp-verb\">se</span><span class=\"vp-prep\">ment</span>: Heureusement, il y a un bus. · vrai → vrai<span class=\"vp-prep\">ment</span>. · constant → const<span class=\"vp-prep\">amment</span>. · Elle chante <span class=\"vp-verb\">bien</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "Most adverbs: <strong>feminine form + -ment</strong>. <em>lent → lente → lentement. heureux → heureuse → heureusement. doux → douce → doucement. complet → complète → complètement.</em>",
        "tag": "feminine + -ment"
      },
      {
        "html": "If the adjective already ends in a vowel, add <strong>-ment</strong> to it as it is: <em>vrai → vraiment, poli → poliment, facile → facilement, rapide → rapidement.</em>",
        "tag": "vowel + -ment"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>-ant</strong> becomes <strong>-amment</strong>, <strong>-ent</strong> becomes <strong>-emment</strong>. Spelling differs, sound is the same, [amɑ̃]: <em>constant → constamment, évident → évidemment, prudent → prudemment.</em> Exception: <em>lent → lentement.</em>",
        "tag": "-amment, -emment"
      },
      {
        "html": "Three adverbs are irregular and do not end in -ment: <strong>bien</strong> (well), <strong>mal</strong> (badly), <strong>vite</strong> (fast). <em>Elle chante bien. Il conduit mal. Elle court vite.</em>",
        "tag": "bien, mal, vite"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>bon / mauvais</strong> describe a noun and agree: <em>un bon médecin, une mauvaise nuit.</em> <strong>bien / mal</strong> describe a verb and never change: <em>Il travaille bien. Elle parle mal anglais. Je ne me sens pas bien.</em> (For <em>meilleur</em> and <em>mieux</em>, see the comparatives.)",
        "tag": "bon / bien"
      },
      {
        "html": "Adverbs never agree. They go right after the verb, between avoir and the participle in the passé composé (<em>Elle a bien dormi</em>), or at the start of a sentence: <em>Heureusement, il y a un bus.</em>",
        "tag": "Position"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>Il parle lentment</em> → ✓ <em>Il parle lentement</em>. ✗ <em>Elle travaille constantement</em> → ✓ <em>Elle travaille constamment</em>. ✗ <em>Elle parle bon français</em> → ✓ <em>Elle parle bien français</em>. ✗ <em>Il conduit mauvais</em> → ✓ <em>Il conduit mal</em>. ✗ <em>C'est un bien médecin</em> → ✓ <em>C'est un bon médecin</em>. Next: <a href=\"/comparatives\">comparatives</a>, <a href=\"/frequency-adverbs\">how often</a>.",
    "reference": {
